1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Meet Aura DCA — an autonomous dollar-cost-averaging agent built on Arc Network.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Its whole idea fits in one line: let Claude drive the strategy, but let code own every number that touches money.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:34,000
A language model is brilliant at judgment — but you can never let it be the final authority on how much money to move.

4
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:45,000
One hallucinated number drains the wallet. And when the money isn't yours, the agent must be fair as well as safe.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Claude recommends — it reads the balance, the budget, and its own history, and proposes an amount with reasoning.

6
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,000
But clampDecision — a pure, unit-tested function — re-derives the real limit from hard guardrails and decides the number actually spent.

7
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Claude is never trusted with the final figure. Two orbits that meet only at the decision.

8
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Now the hard part — serving many users at once. Each wallet sets its own cadence and caps.

9
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Every run, the agent executes the sum as a single swap, then splits the proceeds pro-rata. Nobody subsidises anyone.

10
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:44,000
And this isn't a diagram — it ran on-chain, unsupervised.

11
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Two wallets, one USDC each, pooled into a single 2-USDC swap into EURC, split to the exact total.

12
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Here's that transaction on ArcScan. The swap is real, and the links are the source of truth.

13
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:17,000
It's non-custodial by construction, using both halves of Circle's wallet stack. The agent runs on a Developer-Controlled Wallet.

14
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Users sign in with Google to mint a real Circle User-Controlled Wallet on Arc — no MetaMask, no seed phrase.

15
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:38,000
It even audits itself — recording a hash of its ledger in an Arc Testnet contract only its wallet can write to.

16
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Fourteen-plus attestations, written by the cron with no human involved. The contract holds no funds.

17
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Now the honest part. The cirBTC route went into a liquidity outage — no route available for fourteen straight days.

18
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:06,000
That's the market, not a bug — and we can prove it's isolated to cirBTC.

19
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The agent withheld spend to preserve capital. Knowing when not to act is the harder half.

20
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:21,000
One layer deeper: the agent pays for its own inputs. Over x402, it unlocks a metered brief with a signed USDC authorization.

21
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Cryptographically verified, settlement gated off — an additive module that never touches the DCA path.

22
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Under the hood: TypeScript, Claude, Circle Swap Kit, both wallet types, a Solidity audit anchor, a GitHub Actions cron.

23
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Sixty-five unit tests guard the money path, and live trading needs two switches. Tested code owns the money.

24
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,000
The hard claim is proven, not asserted — many users, one swap, split to the last unit, twice.

25
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Real, verifiable execution. A safety architecture where code owns the money. An agent that audits itself on-chain.

26
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Two independent paths — Claude's judgment and the code's guardrails — meeting at every decision. That's where the safety lives.

27
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:25,000
It's live at aura-dca.xyz, the code is open, and every claim is verifiable on-chain. Thanks for watching.
